@page
@model CompanionModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Android companion";
    ViewData["Section"] = "Settings";
}
<header class="page-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">ON YOUR LOCAL NETWORK</p><h1>Android companion<span class="heading-dot">.</span></h1><p class="lede">Control this computer's games from your phone.</p></div></header>
<div asp-validation-summary="All" class="validation" role="alert"></div>
<section class="panel">
    <h2>LAN access</h2>
    <p>Off by default. Enable only on a trusted home network. Pairing and commands currently use unencrypted HTTP: people able to inspect your network traffic could capture the pairing credential and launch your saved games.</p>
    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Save">
        <label class="check-label"><input asp-for="Enabled" /> Enable Android companion access</label>
        <div class="field"><label asp-for="Port">LAN port</label><input asp-for="Port" type="number" min="1024" max="65535" required /></div>
        <button type="submit" class="button primary">Save companion preferences</button>
    </form>
    <p class="helper">Restart after enabling or changing the port. Disabling blocks new requests immediately and disconnects phones; restart also releases the port. The Windows desktop UI remains private.</p>
    @if (Model.Access.ListenerError is string listenerError)
    {
        <p class="validation" role="alert">@listenerError</p>
    }
    <dl class="settings-list">
        <div><dt>Computer</dt><dd>@Model.Access.Device.Name</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Device ID</dt><dd><code>@Model.Access.DeviceId</code></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Server</dt><dd>@(Model.Access.IsActive ? $"Accepting companion requests on port {Model.Access.ListeningPort}" : "Offline / disabled — restart if you just enabled it")</dd></div>
        <div><dt>IPv4 addresses</dt><dd>@(Model.Addresses.Count == 0 ? "No supported LAN IPv4 address found." : string.Join(" · ", Model.Addresses))</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="helper">Use an address on the same network as your phone, not a VPN/virtual adapter. IPv6-only networks and automatic discovery are not supported in this first version. If your IP changes, pair the same computer at its new address; its device ID stays the same.</p>
</section>
<section class="panel">
    <h2>Pair a phone</h2>
    <p>In the Android app, tap +, enter this computer's IP address and active port, then enter a fresh code from this page.</p>
    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Pair"><button type="submit" class="button primary" disabled="@(!Model.Access.IsActive)">Generate pairing code</button></form>
    @if (Model.PairingCode is string code)
    {
        <div class="provider-status has-key" role="status"><div class="provider-status-copy"><div><span class="provider-status-name">ONE-TIME PAIRING CODE</span><strong>@code</strong><p>Expires at @Model.PairingExpires?.ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm:ss"). One successful pairing or five failed attempts consumes it.</p></div></div></div>
    }
    <p class="helper">Codes expire after two minutes. Generate a new code for each phone. Credentials are separate for each Windows installation and protected at rest for your Windows account.</p>
    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Revoke" data-confirm="Revoke every phone paired with this computer? They will need to pair again."><button type="submit" class="button danger">Revoke all phone pairings</button></form>
</section>
<section class="panel">
    <h2>Windows Firewall</h2>
    <p>Use a Private Windows network profile. If the phone cannot connect, the repository's <code>scripts/Set-CompanionFirewall.ps1</code> can explicitly add a rule from an administrator PowerShell window, scoped to GameLauncher.exe, this TCP port, the Private profile, and LocalSubnet.</p>
    <p class="helper">The installer never opens the firewall automatically. Do not enable public-network access, router port forwarding, or UPnP. This is a local remote control, not an internet service.</p>
    <a class="button secondary" asp-page="/Settings">Back to settings</a>
</section>
